===Etymology===
From {{confix|en|homo|onym}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhɒmənɪm/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhɑmənɪm/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-homonym.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|word with same spelling or same sound or both}} {{lb|en|broadly}} A [[word]] that [[sound#Verb|sounds]] or is [[spell#Etymology 2|spelled]] the same as another word (but not necessarily both).
#: {{hypo|en|homograph<qq:same spelling>|homophone<qq:same sound>}}
## {{senseid|en|word with both same spelling and same sound}} {{lb|en|semantics|strictly}} A word that both sounds and is spelled the same as another word.
## {{lb|en|taxonomy}} A name for a [[taxon]] that is identical in spelling to another name that belongs to a different taxon.

====Usage notes====
* Homonyms (in the broader sense) are divided into the two overlapping subcategories [[homograph]]s and [[homophone]]s. Examples:
** {{m|en|die}} and {{m|en|dye}} (homophones but not homographs)
** the {{m|en|parasitic}} {{m|en|flatworm}} called the {{m|en|fluke}}, the fish called the {{m|en|fluke}}, and a {{m|en|fluke}}, part of the tail of a whale (both homophones and homographs and therefore true homonyms in the strict sense)
** the metal {{m|en|lead}} and the present tense of the verb {{m|en|lead}} (homographs but not homophones)

